{"subjectId":"python","id":"dataclasses-typing","title":"Dataclasses & __slots__","group":"Object-Oriented Python","order":36,"html":"<p class=\"lead\"><code>@dataclass</code> writes the boring methods (<code>__init__</code>, <code>__repr__</code>, <code>__eq__</code>…) from type-annotated fields. <code>__slots__</code> trims per-object memory. Together they make compact, readable data classes.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-wand\"></i>@dataclass basics</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>from dataclasses import dataclass, field\n\n@dataclass(order=True)\nclass Student:\n    name: str\n    marks: int = 0\n    tags: list[str] = field(default_factory=list)   # never a bare [] default\n\ns = Student(\"Asha\", 91)\nprint(s)                                    # Student(name='Asha', marks=91, tags=[])\nprint(s == Student(\"Asha\", 91))             # True\nprint(sorted([Student(\"b\", 2), Student(\"a\", 5)])[0].name)   # a</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>@dataclass</code></td><td>Generates <code>__init__</code>, <code>__repr__</code>, <code>__eq__</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>order=True</code></td><td>Adds <code>&lt;</code>, <code>&lt;=</code>, <code>&gt;</code>, <code>&gt;=</code> comparing fields as a tuple, in order</td></tr><tr><td><code>frozen=True</code></td><td>Assigning a field raises <code>FrozenInstanceError</code>; instances become hashable</td></tr><tr><td><code>slots=True</code></td><td>Uses <code>__slots__</code> — less memory, faster attribute access (3.10+)</td></tr><tr><td><code>kw_only=True</code></td><td>Every field must be passed by keyword (3.10+)</td></tr><tr><td><code>field(default_factory=…)</code></td><td>A fresh mutable default per instance</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>from dataclasses import dataclass\n\ntry:\n    @dataclass\n    class Bad:\n        items: list = []\nexcept ValueError as e:\n    print(e)   # mutable default &lt;class 'list'&gt; for field items is not allowed: use default_factory</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-checks\"></i>__post_init__: validate and derive</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>from dataclasses import dataclass, field\n\n@dataclass\nclass Rectangle:\n    w: float\n    h: float\n    area: float = field(init=False)      # computed — not an __init__ parameter\n\n    def __post_init__(self):             # runs right after the generated __init__\n        if self.w &lt;= 0 or self.h &lt;= 0:\n            raise ValueError(\"sides must be positive\")\n        self.area = self.w * self.h\n\nprint(Rectangle(3, 4))                   # Rectangle(w=3, h=4, area=12)\ntry:\n    Rectangle(0, 4)\nexcept ValueError as e:\n    print(e)                             # sides must be positive</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-snowflake\"></i>Frozen dataclasses &amp; helpers</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>from dataclasses import dataclass, replace, asdict, astuple, FrozenInstanceError\n\n@dataclass(frozen=True)\nclass Point:\n    x: int\n    y: int\n\np = Point(1, 2)\ntry:\n    p.x = 5\nexcept FrozenInstanceError:\n    print(\"frozen\")                        # frozen\nq = replace(p, y=9)                        # copy with some fields changed\nprint(q, len({p, Point(1, 2)}))            # Point(x=1, y=9) 1\nprint(asdict(q), astuple(q))               # {'x': 1, 'y': 9} (1, 9)</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p><code>asdict()</code> recurses into nested dataclasses, lists and dicts — handy right before <code>json.dumps</code>.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-adjustments\"></i>Field options, keyword-only fields &amp; InitVar</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>from dataclasses import dataclass, field, KW_ONLY, InitVar\n\n@dataclass\nclass User:\n    name: str\n    password: str = field(repr=False)                     # hidden from repr\n    login_count: int = field(default=0, compare=False)    # ignored by ==\n    _: KW_ONLY                                            # everything below is keyword-only\n    admin: bool = False\n\na = User(\"asha\", \"s3cret\", 5)\nprint(a)                                  # User(name='asha', login_count=5, admin=False)\nprint(a == User(\"asha\", \"s3cret\", 1, admin=False))   # True\n\n@dataclass\nclass Account:\n    owner: str\n    raw_pin: InitVar[str]                 # passed to __init__ and __post_init__, never stored\n    pin_len: int = field(init=False)\n\n    def __post_init__(self, raw_pin):\n        self.pin_len = len(raw_pin)\n\nacc = Account(\"ravi\", \"1234\")\nprint(acc, hasattr(acc, \"raw_pin\"))       # Account(owner='ravi', pin_len=4) False</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th><code>field(...)</code> argument</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>default</code> / <code>default_factory</code></td><td>Default value / zero-argument callable producing one</td></tr><tr><td><code>init=False</code></td><td>Not an <code>__init__</code> parameter (set it in <code>__post_init__</code>)</td></tr><tr><td><code>repr=False</code></td><td>Left out of <code>__repr__</code> (secrets, huge blobs)</td></tr><tr><td><code>compare=False</code></td><td>Ignored by <code>==</code> and ordering</td></tr><tr><td><code>hash=None</code></td><td>Follows <code>compare</code> for hashing</td></tr><tr><td><code>kw_only=True</code></td><td>This one field is keyword-only</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-scale\"></i>dataclass vs the alternatives</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>Mutable</th><th>Validation</th><th>Best for</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>@dataclass</code></td><td>Yes (or <code>frozen=True</code>)</td><td>Manual, in <code>__post_init__</code></td><td>Plain internal records with behaviour</td></tr><tr><td><code>typing.NamedTuple</code></td><td>No</td><td>None</td><td>Lightweight immutable records that also behave as tuples</td></tr><tr><td><code>TypedDict</code></td><td>Yes (it's a <code>dict</code>)</td><td>Static only</td><td>Typing JSON-like dicts you receive</td></tr><tr><td><code>attrs</code> (3rd-party)</td><td>Yes</td><td>Validators &amp; converters</td><td>Everything dataclasses do, plus more</td></tr><tr><td><code>pydantic</code> (3rd-party)</td><td>Yes</td><td>Full runtime parsing &amp; coercion</td><td>API payloads, configs, untrusted input</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-database\"></i>__slots__</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>from dataclasses import dataclass\n\nclass Point:\n    __slots__ = (\"x\", \"y\")      # fixed attributes, no per-instance __dict__\n    def __init__(self, x, y):\n        self.x, self.y = x, y\n\np = Point(1, 2)\ntry:\n    p.z = 3\nexcept AttributeError:\n    print(\"no new attributes\")  # no new attributes\nprint(hasattr(p, \"__dict__\"))   # False\n\n@dataclass(slots=True)\nclass Pixel:\n    x: int\n    y: int\nprint(Pixel.__slots__)          # ('x', 'y')</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p><code>__slots__</code> saves memory when you create millions of small objects, at the cost of not being able to add attributes dynamically (and no <code>cached_property</code>). Subclasses need their own <code>__slots__</code> to keep the benefit.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-tag\"></i>Enums &amp; type hints in brief</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>from enum import Enum, auto\nfrom typing import Optional\n\nclass Status(Enum):\n    TODO = auto()\n    DONE = auto()\n\ndef find(names: list[str], target: str) -&gt; Optional[int]:\n    return names.index(target) if target in names else None\n\nprint(Status.DONE, Status.DONE.value)       # Status.DONE 2\nprint(find([\"a\", \"b\"], \"b\"), find([], \"x\")) # 1 None</code></pre></figure>\n<p>Both get their own topics: see <strong>Enums in Depth</strong> and <strong>Type Hints &amp; mypy</strong>.</p>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Why does a dataclass refuse items: list = []?</summary><div><p>Defaults are evaluated once, so every instance would share one list — the same bug as a mutable default argument. <code>dataclasses</code> detects <code>list</code>, <code>dict</code> and <code>set</code> defaults and raises <code>ValueError</code>; use <code>field(default_factory=list)</code>.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>When is a dataclass hashable?</summary><div><p>With the default <code>eq=True</code>, <code>__hash__</code> is set to <code>None</code> (unhashable), because mutable objects that compare by value must not be hashed. Use <code>frozen=True</code> (or <code>unsafe_hash=True</code> if you know what you're doing) to get a field-based <code>__hash__</code>.</p></div></details></section>"}